A||||2||deceler8||Excessive spark knock...why ?||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||09-16-2000||10:42 PM||||I have an '86 2m4, automatic...I've been noticing lately that I have a spark knock at even fairly light throttle after the engine is warmed up.<P>I've rechecked timing and it's right at eight degrees. I'm running a 180 thermostat and do not have any overheating issues. Thermac seems to be operating correctly (door is open when engine is warm)...I've also replaced the ECM temp sensor.<P>There is vacuum going to the EGR, but I can't check actual operation of the EGR as it's one of those new ones which can't be manually operated (can't feel underneath for movement or see operation either).<P>And I'm running 89 octane gas with ethanol (as any good Iowa boy would).<P>Does this sound like a bad EGR...not opening?<P>Any way to check whether this valve is good either on the engine or off ?<P>As usual, thanks for the replies.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||09-16-2000||11:15 PM||||You might try hooking up a hand vacuum pump to the egr valve.  Start the engine and let it idle.  Apply vacuum to the valve with the hand pump.  The engine should stumble and stall if the egr valve is opening.  If it doesn't then either the valve is bad or the passage in the cylinder head or intake manifold is clogged.  If the valve checks out OK, then your problem may be a carbon build up in the cylinders.  It doesn't take much to raise the compression enough to get some detonation.  Another possibility might be spark plugs that are too high a heat range for this engine.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||09-16-2000||11:27 PM||||pull the plugs and check the gap. wrong gap can cause problems.<P>I dont think EGR on these motors will open with just a vacuum pump. Check the Haynes book on that one.<P>Try some diferant gas. Gas formulas change all the time. what's in there now may not be the same as a few weeks or days ago.<P>Check for vac leaks. Check the lines to the vapor can, especially the small one. A slight vac leak could lean it out. The small line to the vapor can would not affect idle. It's tied to the TBI such that it only pulls vacuum as the throttle is opened.||||reg||1
